1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 Let's have a very short discussion on the effects of inflation. I do not consider that to be a major aspect of the course, but I think it's good that we cover it briefly.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
 So what is the impact of inflation and how do we deal with it in our computations?

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 So what is inflation? Well, inflation means that our purchasing power is going down.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
 That means that we need more money to obtain the same amount of a product or service before inflation was there.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 Or we could say that our dollar, euro value is decreasing.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 So it's not the same as the time value of money. It's something that comes on top of that. So maybe that's important to realize and to know.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 So we are working with an inflation rate, F, so that's the percent, is a percent per time period.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 So it's handled in the same way as a regular interest rate.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 So for the example, if we have an inflation rate of 5% per year, then $100 today will require $105 to buy the same amount next year.

10
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 Or, well, you see there a formula representing this calculation.

11
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 So again, inflation is on top of the interest rate.

12
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 Assume that the firm desires to purchase an asset that is costing $209,000 in today's dollars.

13
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 Assume a reasonable interest rate of 4 per year, then that same piece of equipment would cost quite a lot more.

14
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 So it's not an interest rate or a rate of return. Again, let's make that clear.

15
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 There are three important rates. We have the real or inflation free interest rate.

16
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 So that's the rate at which interest is earned. The effect of inflation has been removed.

17
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 So it represents actual or real gain received charge on investments or borrowed funds.

18
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 Then we have the market rate, which is the inflation adjusted interest rate.

19
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
 And then we have the pure interest rate.

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 So here we can start making computations. I think they are pretty straightforward.

21
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 So we have an example where we have the $5,000 now inflated at 4% per year at an interest rate of 10%.

22
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 So we see the computations in the table where we have the cost increased due to the inflation.

23
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 So then we have the cost in future dollars, the future cost in constant dollars, and then the present worth.

24
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
 So I think they are pretty straightforward computations.

25
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 So we can maybe try to combine them.

26
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 So we start with the real interest rate, I, and assume that F is a future dollar amount with inflation built in.

27
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 So that means that we have V is F divided by 1 plus I to the power n, as we usually have.

28
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 But you have to combine it with an inflation rate F.

29
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 So we have to, as we treat it in exactly the same way as we treat a regular interest rate, we will divide by 1 plus F to the power n.

30
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,000
 So that means that we will have 1 plus F times 1 plus I.

31
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 That is giving us 1 plus I plus F plus IF as the new factor that we were looking for.

32
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
 And we call this I plus F plus I times IF, we call I subscript F, which is the market rate.

33
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 And so we can use it in our formulas, in our regular factor formulas that we have,

34
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 as we use a regular interest rate, but you have to know that it's the market rate.

35
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 And then we can start to use that IF to compute future worth of actual amounts.

36
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:28,000
 You see the formulas, you see the calculations that go with those formulas.

37
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:36,000
 I will leave it at your own discretion to try out these things. They're really pretty straightforward.

38
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:46,000
 What is important to note maybe is that inflation will have an impact on our capital recovery calculations.

39
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:53,000
 Actually, we will need more dollars if inflation is there.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 As you see in the example, so if a small company invests 150,000 in a production line machine,

41
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
 how much does it receive every year to recover the investment in five years?

42
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 The real interest rate is 10 percent, we are familiar with that.

43
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
 And the inflation rate is 4 percent.

44
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 Then we could not just, we cannot just use the 10 percent we have.

45
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
 We really have to take into account the 4 percent as well.

46
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 So we have to calculate our new interest rate to use in our formula for our factor.

47
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 And so we don't have, we have IF is 0.10 plus 0.04 plus 0.10 times 0.04, which makes 14.4.

48
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 And which has an impact on our capital recovery.

49
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
 So it's not without any consequence.

50
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:53,000
 Normally, inflation is like 2 to 8 percent per year.

51
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 Some countries, especially between currencies or unstable political situation, can have higher hyperinflation.

52
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 And the only good advice if you have hyperinflation is to spend your money as quickly as possible because it's losing value very quickly.

53
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:27,000
 It's also very difficult then to talk about engineering economy calculations because, well, the situation is very unreliable, unstable, uncertain.

54
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:35,000
 A few simple exercises that we can deal with later on.

55
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 So very short chapter.

56
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 As I said, it's definitely not the core of the course, but we need to know something about it.

57
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:54,000
 We need to know that sometimes we need to adjust our regular interest rate with a correction, if you want to call it that way, for inflation.

